-based administration. Crucially, it says: \"A közigazgatásban ettől az időponttól digitálisan kell kiadni mindent illetve minden kérelmet így kell fogadni.\" (From this point on, everything must be issued digitally in public administration, and all requests must be received this way.) This strongly implies mandatory digital communication for administrative bodies, which would include courts.\n\nSearch Result 9 (Hungarian Code of Civil Procedure) mentions electronic service (elektronikus kézbesítés) as increasingly used for registered users of the judicial system. This supports the idea of electronic communication but doesn't specify DÁP as the mandatory channel for summonses.\n\nSearch Result 17 (A digitális állampolgárság) reiterates that eDokumentumkezelés (e-document management) and eFizetés (e-payment) services are to be provided from January 1, 2026. This aligns with Search Result 8.\n\nSearch Result 16 (Dáptv. Vhr. - 321/2024. (XI. 6.) Korm. rendelet a digitális ...) refers to a government decree related to digital services, but the snippet doesn't provide specific details about court summonses via DÁP.\n\nBased on Search Result 11, it appears that by 2026, digital issuance and receipt of all administrative matters will be mandatory for public administration bodies. The DÁP application is identified as the entry point for this digital communication. While the specific law mandating courts to send summonses *exclusively* via DÁP in all circumstances is not explicitly stated with a direct quote, the general obligation for public administration to conduct matters digitally from 2026, with DÁP as the entry point, strongly suggests this is the intended mechanism. The law mentioned in Search Result 8 (2023. évi CIII. törvény) and the government decree in Search Result 16 are likely the foundational legal acts. Search Result 11, a government announcement, clarifies the implementation timeline and scope, indicating that by 2026, digital issuance and receipt will be t